{"qid":"test_Psychology_83","dataset":"mmmupro","question":"The observation notes below were used as part of a naturalistic observation study of a group of middle school students. All names are pseudonyms. Context: During the 10 minutes of this observation, this group of four middle school students worked to complete a project assigned by their English teacher. The four students were seated around a table, which was covered with construction paper, scissors, glue sticks, and their open textbooks. The students' task was to make a creative display that communicates their feelings about the end of the school year. All observation entries are time stamped, student comments are in quotation marks, and student actions are in brackets.   Look at the information from the naturalistic observation notes about Guy. Which of the following motivation principles seems to fit Guy's experience best, based solely on the information you find in these observation notes?\nOptions:\nA: Self-determination theory\nB: Intrinsic motivation\nC: Achievement motivation\nD: Maslow's hierarchy of needs\nE: Yerkes-Dodson law\nF: Extrinsic motivation\nG: Drive reduction theory\nH: Incentive theory\nI: Law of effect\nJ: Homeostasis motivation\nAnswer with the option's letter.","gold":"E","category":"Psychology","generated_text":"To determine which motivation principle best fits Guy\u2019s experience based on the observation notes, let\u2019s analyze his statements:\n\n- At 12:11, Guy says: \u201cWay better than mine [sighs]. I keep screwing this thing up; I think I\u2019m too nervous. I do OK sometimes when I\u2019m just a bit nervous, but I\u2019m way too stressed right now, so I don\u2019t think I can do this right.\u201d\n\nKey points from Guy\u2019s statement:\n- He is comparing his work to others\u2019 (\u201cWay better than mine\u201d) \u2014 indicating he is concerned with performance relative to peers.\n- He expresses self-doubt and anxiety (\u201cI keep screwing this thing up,\u201d \u201cI think I\u2019m too nervous,\u201d \u201cI\u2019m way too stressed\u201d).\n- He acknowledges that he can do OK under some conditions (\u201cwhen I\u2019m just a bit nervous\u201d), but is currently overwhelmed.\n- His focus is on avoiding failure and achieving correctness (\u201cI don\u2019t think I can do this right\u201d).\n\nNow, let\u2019s evaluate the options:\n\nA: Self-determination theory \u2014 This is a broad theory about intrinsic vs. extrinsic motivation and the role of autonomy, competence, and relatedness. While Guy\u2019s stress might relate to competence, the theory is too broad for this specific context.\n\nB: Intrinsic motivation \u2014 This refers to doing something for inherent satisfaction. Guy\u2019s statements suggest he is not enjoying the task; he is anxious and focused on avoiding failure. This does not fit.\n\nC: Achievement motivation \u2014 This refers to the drive to succeed and avoid failure, often involving a strong concern for performance and evaluation. Guy\u2019s anxiety, self-doubt, and focus on doing \u201cthis right\u201d strongly align with achievement motivation. He is motivated by the desire to succeed and avoid failure, even if it\u2019s causing him stress.\n\nD: Maslow\u2019s hierarchy of needs \u2014 This is about basic needs (physiological, safety, belonging, esteem, self-actualization). Guy\u2019s stress is not about unmet basic needs but about performance anxiety.\n\nE: Yerkes-Dodson law \u2014 This describes the relationship between arousal and performance, suggesting optimal performance at moderate arousal. Guy is experiencing high stress, which may be impairing his performance \u2014 but this is a law about arousal levels, not a motivation principle per se.\n\nF: Extrinsic motivation \u2014 This is motivation driven by external rewards or avoiding punishment. While Guy might be concerned with grades (as seen in Carter\u2019s comment), Guy himself does not mention external rewards or punishments. His motivation is internal (fear of failure, desire to do well).\n\nG: Drive reduction theory \u2014 This is about reducing internal tension (e.g., hunger, thirst). Guy\u2019s stress is not a drive he is trying to reduce; it\u2019s a source of anxiety.\n\nH: Incentive theory \u2014 This is about being motivated by external rewards or incentives. Again, Guy is not focused on external rewards.\n\nI: Law of effect \u2014 This is about learning through reinforcement. Not applicable here.\n\nJ: Homeostasis motivation \u2014 This is about maintaining internal balance. Guy\u2019s stress is not about restoring balance.\n\nThe best fit is **C: Achievement motivation**, because Guy is clearly motivated by the desire to succeed and avoid failure, which is the core of achievement motivation. His anxiety and self-doubt are typical of high achievement motivation, where the fear of failure can be intense.\n\nFinal answer: C","n_input":1088,"n_generated":697,"seq_len":1785,"grid":[18,45],"img_span":[4,814],"user_text_span":[815,1083],"answer_seg":[695,696],"ans_tp":[1782],"texts":["<|im_start|>","user","\n","<|vision_start|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pa